A||N||23||BellaFiero||A gift for everyone on the Forum - Hope you like it!||||7||
Z||000000||BellaFiero||09-14-2000||10:36 AM||||By Jason Friesner ( BellaFiero )<BR>(Please do not copy without permission)<P><BR>Dead Man’s Curve had actually taken a life. The body was gone, but a  corpse did remained. There had been a spin, tires screeched and blew, and the road had faded into forest. The car had rolled, and slid…  the driver left in the brush, the car had continued down the slope.<BR>Over years, the forest had reclaimed the path the heavy body had made. The cavern which housed her rusting frame had grown closed with forest, thick branches and bush had hidden her existence from the outside world. In a cold, damp hole she had spent the years. <BR>The sky was heavy and dark with storm, and the rain fell in thick, cold sheets. Fall’s storms had begun. The lightning crackled, hissing in heat and electric pain across the night air. Hungry, it sought for metal, for a conductor, to continue the current, to breathe life into it’s seconds-of-existence. It found the frame, hissing through leaves and branches, using the water to guide itself. <BR>The blown tires, the rims sunk in the ground and rock, sparked as the lightning struck.<BR>Her growl was soft, filling the tiny cavern with the hissing of pistons and a damaged exhaust. The rev became harder, the tachometer’s needle burying itself as she shifted to nuetral. Headlights rose slowly, teetering on rusty motors, decades of motionless sitting aching in her frame. The engine howled once, twice, and the cavern shook with the force. The moonlight outside the cave sought it’s way into the darkness, silvery light shedding shadow. Her wheels rolled forward, the flat tire on the driver’s side hissing as it sucked air back in through the ragged hole. Fibers and rubber melted back together, and the exhaust’s loud rumble silenced as the metal forged itself back into one piece. Headlights locked into positon as she shifted into first gear. The tires bit gravel, throwing rocks against the back of the cave, and the red  of the brake illuminated the stone walls in bloody light as she slowed at the entrance. Thick woods covered her escape.<BR>The engine revved up, screaming now,  and the tires spun in the water. The branches and leaves exploded as shrapnel, as if a bomb had exploded in the middle of the woods. The 1985 Fiero GT howled like a dead ghost as she climbed her way back up the hill, fighting the water and the slick grass. The front fascia tore into the shrubs, shredding all in her path.<BR>The road lay ahead. The yellow and white lines of home. The familiar black tar. She was almost there. The engine moaned in satisfaction as the front tires gripped the road, and the back forced her onto the slick pavement. The bright lights of the Fiero narrowed, sliding down on their motors, viscious eyes of an angry predator in an industrial jungle. Fords and Toyotas shot by, the highway home to a myriad of aerodynamically shaped cars, of new technology and new styles. The Fiero watched the passing cars as she sat on the side of the road. <BR>The lights returned to their full upright position, and the tires spun. She gripped the road and blazed her way into the darkness of the oncoming night.<BR>As she drove, the bumper stickers of her previous owner fell off, and the window tint darkened to pitch. The sun roof, cracked and in pieces in the passenger seat, gripped to one another, turning darkest black, and suddenly popped down into the hole. The heater core kicked on, and the vents aimed at the seats, drying the rotted leather from years of weathering.<BR>Across the front of the car’s windshield, as she saw the windows banners and stickers of the ‘sports’ cars beside her, appeared in bloody scrawl two words. “Cauldron Born.”<BR>A 1986 Corvette shot past on the passing lane, neon lights and booming music causing the Fiero GT to slow down. The lights narrowed, ratcheting down to thin slits of hatred, and the tires pushed her faster. The throttle roared, and the Fiero shot towards the Vette.<BR>Much as a wolf chases the deer.<BR>The rain hardened, and the night stretched on.<BR>On the side of the road, rolled and broken, the police and fire department would find an ’86 Vette. Across the side would be a smear of red paint, whose factory color they would trace back to…<BR>A  Pontiac. A1985 Fiero GT. <BR>Cauldron Born.<BR>A Honda Civic, striped and riced, rocketed past the curve, the loud angry exhaust note of the bumble bee muffler filling the highway with the sound of thunder. Strobing break lights flared as the driver braked for the turn…<BR>… Up ahead, a metal and plastic slash of red waited, slowing down, revving it’s engine in challenge.<BR>The Honda driver turned his hat backwards and smiled over at his girlfriend, just before they pulled up alongside the two seater. The sunroof popped up on the Fiero, and the sound of Hart’s “Baraccuda” screamed out over the Civic’s Notorious B.I.G. CD.<BR>The engines howled.<P><BR>To Be Continued ( Based on Forum enthusiasm )<BR>___________________<BR>Jayse<BR>1988 "Bella Donna" Custom<BR>1985 Fiero GT "Cauldron Born" ( saved from the U-Wrench-It and currently being reborn )<BR>||||reg||7
