Z||000017||BellaFiero||03-14-2001||11:04 AM||||Wow... you guys do like it.. awesome. Tell you what, I'll write up another chapter here in a second and post it... if you guys still like the stories, I'll turn this into a weekly thing.. maybe post every Friday. I think I'll have a little more fun with my Cauldron Born for a while before I introduce a hero or mortal character  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> By the way, Cauldron Born is the name of my 1985 GT that I've restored from the dead. I've had a 88 coupe Fiero since I was 17, and now that I'm 21 I've actually got some of the free-time ( work and school can really hamper a shade-tree mechanic's time! ) to work on the things I love best. At any rate, I found this dead 85 in a newspaper. It just had the engine re-built ( odometer reads 69,120 ) but it was in pieces - the owner was an old woman who ran out of money to finish fixing her car, so she had it brought back to her house. She never got around to it, so dang it - I sure did! $700 bucks and five weekends later, I've got a super-clean, low-mileage, optioned-out GT!  It's really reliable for sitting so long - I have had to replace just about every hose. I ceramic-coated the manifolds and installed Ansa resonators on her, just finished an all-Alpine stereo install, and am now ravaging the streets of Saint Louis! Let me know if you want the stories to keep coming!! ||||reg||1
Z||000018||BellaFiero||03-14-2001||12:23 PM||||<BR>Leaves fluttered with the stirring breezes that raced in through the valley, blanketing the natural walls with icy gail. The heady, dusky scent of thunderclouds mingled with the cold winds. The night was heavy with storm, a thick black pregnant with lightning and rain. <BR>	The headlights, dim and narrow, ratcheted up only a few inches like the eyes of a lion rising above the grass-line of it’s savanna. Tires creapt across gravel and grass as pipes hissed with heat, moldings returning and brackets searing to hold one more. Cracked glass, spider-webbed and ready to fall in upon itself, slowly pressed outward, and the darkness inside the compartment pressed into every fracture. In a the space of a few seconds, only the cold sheen of a flawless, tinted window could be seen. <BR>	The heat in the engine bay began to subside as the roaring growl of rage faded, the engine falling off as the shifter eased out of neutral and into drive. The shriek of twisting, repairing pipes died away leaving only the faint lingering feminine scream. That too, quieted, as the car rolled ever closer to the field.<BR>	The modern domestic and imported cars continued with their blaring beat, the loud heavy heart-beats of the rave music carrying out into the valley. Very few had heard the strange growling and screaming coming from the ditch near the field, music and conversation to loud to pick up on the hissing of anti-freeze or the revving of the six cylinders. The muscle cars were left abandoned, the majority of their owners and passengers clustered around the rear of a large black van. Inside, boxes upon boxes sat. Money was changing hands, and boxes were being carried away. Inside, the stolen goods plundered from the parts store rattled as they were shoved into trunks and back seats. Two of the young men running the van looked up at the highway, nervous apprehension obvious in their faces as they strained to see any approaching cops. Money was stuffed into their baggy pants’ pockets. <BR>	The slash of red moved up slowly onto the street, climbing out o fthe ditch as fiberglass pushed itself back to form, paint chips melted and sealed back intot he pattern. The bright red drip of blood on it’s left mirror seemingly evaporated away… or perhaps sucked into the black material.<BR>	With a sound like a yowling cougar, the car impossibly reared up on it’s back wheels, rocking itself fully out of the ditch and onto the highway, front tires hitting the ground as the rear bit into street and pushed. Rubber melted as the tach dumped back, and the squeal of anger was louder than any car stereo.<BR>	The kids looked up at they saw the two-seater rocket towards them, a knife swathing across highway and grassy field. Several ran for their cars, worried of police or angry parents come for young girls. Several joints hit the ground as car keys were scrambled for.<BR>	Not that it mattered much. The red Fiero slammed into a pearl-white Integra, front bumper unexplainably ripping through the body of the foreign car. Only a gash in the fascia material showed any damage to the Pontiac. The engine hit high again, and the tires threw grass and rock into the air behind it, a shark making wakes. <BR>	A group of boys armed with tire-irons tried to convince themselves they could stop the car, and then leaped to the safety of their cars as the headlights turned their brights on, the car centering on them. One of the boys tripped over a too-large wallet chain that hung to his ankle. He moaned as he sat to his knees, rubbing his head. Light flared behind him, silhouetting his form. Kids and young adults watched in horror as the body, black outlined in white, tried to raise to it’s knees… and was subsequently flung to the side as the car sped and braked, swerving it’s side door into the boy’s back. He rolled twice, still breathing, but broken.<BR>	“Why!?”, cried one of the girls at the car as her boyfriend reversed her and her friends away in his Firebird.<BR>	The Fiero’s wheels spun, swinging the headlights to bear on the girl. The engine revved up, but the car did not move. It slowly traced the scene of fleeing kids with it’s brights, lights narrowed to slits. The black van, it’s doors open, sat abandoned, the promise of quick money forgotten. Boxes of…<BR>	…. Sustenance …<BR>sat in the van’s back doors. The car lined itself up with the van, and the engine gunned. Wheels spun and the car raced towards the van, plowing into the back of the old wheeled-box with a grinding screech. The van and the car sped towards the edge of the field, towards the drop-off. <BR>	They watched as the Fiero, firmyl embedded in the van, flung itself from the field and into the darkness of the night. The lights turned off as water surged over the vehicles, sinking slowly in the ocean below.<BR>	The police would attribute it to an angry, scorned child copy-catting school shootings and murders. Most kids spoke of it in only hushed whisper, of some phantom car that came from nowhere, broke Jimmy’s arm, and trashed an Integra before carrying Corey’s van into the sea. There was no sign of the vehicles in the sea, and most of the police dismissed it as hysterics. <BR>	On the coast below, rolling back into a welcome shade of tree-line and rocks, a horrible twisted form that creeped through sand and earth. The sun-roof was missing, both windows smashed, and the hood was gone to leave the front compartment naked and empty. In the empty space, there now sat twin boxes of quart-contained fluids, gallon containers and brake-lines, hoses and braces… <BR>The Cauldron Born flashed in the sunlight as she slept. When the sun fell into the sea once more, the boxes would be gone, and the engine would not moan or cry.<BR>She would roar.<BR>||||reg||11
